WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:06.880
I'm Beatriz.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.210
I came all the way from
Brazil to talk to you

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.120
about how to make awesome
automations with GitHub

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.590
Actions.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.590
So anyone here
has to run scripts

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.490
like every day, every
week, every month.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:28.660
If you do that, GitHub Actions
probably will be useful to you.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.210
So I'm here to talk about
what is GitHub Actions

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.510
and how can we run
R scripts with it.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:40.360
So GitHub Actions
has been heavily used

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.500
by R packages
package developers

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.390
and for running checks on
packages, building the package

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.340
website and documentation.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.950
And if you are a
package developer,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.320
you can easily do
that using package

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.570
usethis, which is
my favorite package.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.780
And you can also see a
lot of other examples

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.410
in this awesome r-lib
actions repository.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:12.080
So how important is GitHub
Actions to RStudio and Posit?

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:19.780
So I look up in the GitHub
um, action API, GitHub API

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.570
to get the action run.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.930
And we can see that in two
years the runs completed runs

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.630
for these, these
organizations, which

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.690
stores our favorite
packages, increase it

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.820
more than four times.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.870
So GitHub Actions is really
important for RStudio and Posit

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.550
to run checks on R packages
and build the documentation

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.300
and stuff.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.570
But I told you about what
package developers can do.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.730
But most people that I know
are not package developers.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:02:00.220
So I'm here to talk about guys,
how you can use that as well.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.540
I've been using it two
years and in the beginning

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.750
it was a bit hard for me.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:09.930
So I'm trying to facilitate
this process, right?

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.310
So we can run arbitrary
R scripts, like scrape

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.630
data from the internet.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.560
I do that a lot, but the
government data. Run reports

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.310
with R Markdown, in Quarto.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.670
We can send emails
and save results all

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.440
doing GitHub Actions with R.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.990
So this is a simple
example of workflow file,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:36.010
but I don't expect you to
understand this right now.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:40.320
But the point is this first
part, we have the events

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.840
and this part tells when
the workflow should run.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.180
In this case, we have
a button to press.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.170
So when we press a button,
we start the workflow

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.820
and also we have a schedule.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.350
So I'm telling the computer
that it has to be run every day.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.900
The second part is the
name of the workflow,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.780
and the third part is what
the workflow should do.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.400
So this part of the jobs part.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.370
Imagine that it's a virtual
machine that belongs to GitHub.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.260
There is nothing
installed and you

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.050
have to tell what
it should, what

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.240
should be installed in
order to run our script

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.800
and what should be done.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.770
So I'm telling that I want
to run a Ubuntu machine.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.870
You can also use like Windows
machine and Apple machines,

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:31.050
macOS machines. And
I'm installing R,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:36.670
but this is super easy because
of the r-lib action repository.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.270
There is a setup there
that we just use it.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.520
And then I'm saying that I
run I want to run my awesome R

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.910
script in the end.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.840
And remember that part.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.840
You have to install
all the packages there,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.700
otherwise it won't
work, it will break.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.590
So this is a really quick
presentation and I prepared

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.470
more examples in my
blog and I couldn't.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.120
I hope I will
continue to do that.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.870
So you can see more
examples there.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.350
I would like to thank especially
RStudio for the diversity

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.200
scholarship.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.150
Otherwise I couldn't be here.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.890
R Ladies, Curso-R.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.820
The creators and contributors
of our r-lib/actions

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.209
and Articulation.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.290
And you can find me on Twitter
and my blog is Bea Milz dot com.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.100
Thank you.

